{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Split the conjunction and use `AddSubmonoid.closure` as the common organizing object.\n\n- For the first conjunct, write the linear set as \\(s=a+P\\), where \\(P\\) is a finitely generated additive submonoid. Then\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{cl}(s)=\\{0\\}\\cup\\bigl(a+\\operatorname{cl}(P\\cup\\{a\\})\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  The second piece is linear because adjoining one generator preserves finite generation; the singleton is linear; hence their finite union is semilinear. This is precisely the mechanism behind `IsLinearSet.closure`. [Mathlib semilinear-set documentation](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/ModelTheory/Arithmetic/Presburger/Semilinear/Defs.html)\n\n- For the second conjunct, put \\(H=\\operatorname{cl}(s)\\). A Frobenius number is the greatest element of \\(\\mathbb N\\setminus H\\). If `setGcd s = 1`, the numerical-semigroup/conductor lemma gives some \\(N\\) such that every \\(m\\ge N\\) lies in \\(H\\). The condition \\(1\\notin s\\) implies \\(1\\notin H\\), so the complement is finite and nonempty and therefore has a maximum. Conversely, a Frobenius number makes \\(H\\) cofinite. Since `setGcd s` divides every element of \\(H\\), it divides two consecutive sufficiently large elements and must equal \\(1\\). Finally, \\(1\\in s\\) would make \\(H=\\mathbb N\\), leaving no gap. This is packaged as `exists_frobeniusNumber_iff`. [Mathlib Frobenius-number documentation](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/NumberTheory/FrobeniusNumber.html)\n\nThe exclusion \\(1\\notin s\\) is essential only to ensure that the complement has an element whose maximum can serve as a natural-valued Frobenius number."}}
